@inject IJSRuntime Js

@*
    A list of tasks that can be reordered.

    The drag is the browser's, and everything about where a row would land is
    held here — which row is moving, which one it is over — because a row cannot
    know either on its own. The list raises one event when the move settles:
    where the row went. Reordering the data is the host's, since the host is the
    only thing that knows whether the order survives a reload.

    Reordering is also on the keyboard, and that is not decoration. A drag is a
    pointer gesture with no keyboard equivalent at all, so a list that only
    dragged would be a list some people cannot reorder. Alt+Arrow moves the
    focused row, and the focus goes with it — the row is a different element
    after the list re-renders, so it is found again by id.

    A move is also said out loud. The visible answer to "where did it go" is the
    row having moved, which is no answer at all to somebody who cannot see the
    list, so each move announces the position it landed in.

    When rows name the ids they wait on, the list works out what that comes to
    and draws it: which rows are blocked, what each of them is waiting for, which
    ones could be started, and which one of those to pick up next. In a linear
    chain the last two are the same row and only one marker appears. They come
    apart in a fan-out, where finishing one step frees several at once — and that
    is exactly when the reader needs telling, because otherwise the unlock is
    visible only by three waiting lines disappearing.

    Worked out, not written down — the Tasks it was
    handed are the Tasks the host still has, and the same answer is available to
    the host from TaskChain without rendering anything. A list where nobody
    declared a dependency derives nothing at all, so its markup is exactly what
    it was before chains existed.

    Quick edit is retitling as one keystroke per row: Tab settles the rename in
    the field and opens the field on the next row, Shift+Tab walks back up. Which
    row that is has to be held here, exactly as the selected row is, because a
    row asked "who is after you" cannot see the rows around it to answer. The
    rename itself stays entirely the row's — the draft, the trimming, and the rule
    that an empty or unchanged title is not a rename are all where they already
    were, so a row tabbed straight through reports nothing.

    The chain runs down the rows on offer and never wraps. Completed rows are not
    in it, because they are not in the list the reader is tabbing down; and coming
    off the last row does not start again at the top, since that would quietly hand
    the reader back the row they began with, retitled once already. Off unless
    asked for: Tab means "the next control" everywhere else in the product.

    Off the bottom of the chain is the add row, which is a control and not a mode.
    It is drawn from the moment the list is, whenever a host is listening for new
    tasks, and it sits in the tab order at the end of the open rows like any other
    field on the page — so the way into it is Tab, from the row before it, with no
    script involved. That is the difference between an add row and the transient
    draft this used to be: a thing that only exists part-way through a keystroke
    chain is a thing a reader cannot find, and a chain that conjures its own last
    step has to invent an exit from it too.

    The exit is now that there is nothing to exit. Tab on an empty add field is
    left entirely to the browser — the focus goes on to whatever follows the list,
    which is what Tab does everywhere — and the only keystroke this list takes off
    the browser is a forward Tab with something typed in the field. That is also
    what stops a held-down Tab from filling the list with rows nobody named: the
    second Tab has an empty field to leave.

    What is typed is this view's own state and never a row in Tasks, exactly like
    the drag preview. A composer nobody finished therefore leaves nothing behind:
    the host is never handed a blank task it did not ask for and then has to go and
    delete, which is what putting a real empty row into the data would have cost.
    Only the title goes out, as OnAdd, on the terms OnRename and OnReorder already
    set — the list says what happened and the host decides what it means.

    With nobody listening to OnAdd there is no add row at all, and Tab off the last
    row commits and stops exactly as it did before any of this existed. Whether a
    list offers to add tasks is the host's answer, and a field whose title nobody
    would hear is the same broken promise as a pencil with no OnRename behind it.
*@

<ul class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId" aria-label="@AriaLabel">
    @if (Tasks.Count == 0)
    {
        <li class="task-list__empty">@EmptyMessage</li>
    }

    @* Preview, not Tasks. While a drag is in flight the rows are shown in the
       order they would end up in, so the gap the row would drop into is a gap
       you can see rather than a line you have to interpret. @key is what makes
       that work: the rows move rather than being rebuilt, so the one under the
       pointer stays the one under the pointer. *@
    @foreach (var task in Open)
    {
        @RenderRow(task)
    }

    @* A row in the markup and not in the data, and there for as long as the list
       is: the place you add tasks is a control the reader can find, not a state the
       list is briefly in. Nothing on it but the field — a circle would offer to
       complete a task that does not exist, a copy button would copy nothing, and a
       pencil would open the editor that is already open.

       Named twice over, because it is the one field in this list with no title to
       borrow: the label is what a screen reader says, and the same words as
       placeholder are what everybody else reads. One word from the host for one
       thing, rather than asking twice for the same answer.

       Keyed so that the element survives the list growing above it. Adding a task
       re-renders the rows in front of this one, and an unkeyed last child is the
       one Blazor is entitled to reuse for something else — which would take the
       caret out of the field mid-sentence. *@
    @if (CanAdd)
    {
        <li @key="_addFieldId" class="task-item task-item--add" data-testid="@Suffix("add")">
            <div class="task-item__rename">
                <TextField Value="@_addTitle"
                           ValueChanged="value => _addTitle = value"
                           AriaLabel="@AddLabel"
                           Placeholder="@AddLabel"
                           Bare="true"
                           InputCssClass="task-item__rename-input"
                           TestId="@Suffix("add-input")"
                           Id="@_addFieldId"
                           @onkeydown="OnAddKeyAsync"
                           @onblur="CommitAddAsync" />
            </div>
        </li>
    }
</ul>

@* The host's own place in the list, under the open rows and above the finished
   ones.

   It exists because "add one more" belongs at the end of the work rather than at
   the end of the document: this list owns the Completed section, so a control the
   host rendered after the whole component landed underneath a fold of things
   already done. A slot rather than an AddLabel parameter — what a host adds, and
   what it calls it, is not a list this library can finish — and an unfilled one
   renders nothing at all, so the markup is exactly what it was before the slot
   existed. *@
@AfterOpenRows

@* Where the row landed, for anyone who cannot see that it moved. Always
   rendered rather than appearing with the first move: a live region has to be in
   the DOM before it changes, or the first announcement is the one nobody
   hears. *@
<p class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-testid="@Suffix("announcement")">@_announcement</p>

@* Finished work goes to the bottom, behind a count you can fold away. A list
   whose done rows stay where they were becomes a list you have to read past to
   find the work — and deleting them instead would throw away the record of what
   was done. Folded by default: the reason to open this is to undo something. *@
@if (GroupCompleted && Completed.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="task-list__completed" data-testid="@CompletedTestId">
        <button type="button"
                class="task-list__completed-header"
                aria-expanded="@(_showCompleted ? "true" : "false")"
                data-testid="@Suffix("completed-toggle")"
                @onclick="() => _showCompleted = !_showCompleted">
            <span class="task-list__twisty" aria-hidden="true">@(_showCompleted ? "⌄" : "›")</span>
            <span>@CompletedLabel</span>
            <span class="task-list__completed-count">@Completed.Count</span>
        </button>

        @if (_showCompleted)
        {
            <ul class="task-list" aria-label="@($"{CompletedLabel}, {AriaLabel}")">
                @foreach (var task in Completed)
                {
                    @RenderRow(task)
                }
            </ul>
        }
    </div>
}

@code {
    private string? _dragging;
    private string? _over;
    private bool _showCompleted;

    /// <summary>What to say about the last keyboard move, if there was one.</summary>
    private string? _announcement;

    /// <summary>The row element to put the focus back on once the host has
    /// applied a keyboard move, or null when nothing is waiting for it.</summary>
    private string? _focusAfterMove;

    /// <summary>
    /// Distinguishes this list's row ids from another list's.
    /// <para>
    /// Two lists on one page can hold the same task ids — an open list and an
    /// archive of the same work is the ordinary case — and an id is unique to a
    /// document rather than to a component. Without this the two lists would mint
    /// the same ids and a move in one could put the focus in the other.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private readonly string _listId = "task-list-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");

    /// <summary>The row with the editor open, while quick edit is on. Held here
    /// rather than in the row for the same reason the drag state is: the fact a
    /// Tab needs is which row comes after this one, and only the list has the
    /// other rows. The row still owns the draft inside it.</summary>
    private string? _editingId;

    /// <summary>The field the add row uses. One element for the life of the list,
    /// which is what makes the guard on it a thing that can be armed once.</summary>
    private readonly string _addFieldId = "task-add-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");

    /// <summary>What has been typed into the add row and not yet added. Held here
    /// and nowhere else — it is a view thing, like the drag preview, so a title
    /// nobody finished is not a change anybody has to undo.</summary>
    private string _addTitle = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The add field is owed the caret: the chain has just come off the
    /// last row, or a title has just gone out and the next one is expected in the
    /// same place. Once, after the render, and never on the first one — a list that
    /// grabbed the focus as the page drew it would take it off whatever the reader
    /// was actually doing.</summary>
    private bool _focusAdd;

    /// <summary>Whether the browser has been told which Tab the add field keeps.
    /// Once is enough and once is all it gets: the listener lives on an element that
    /// is never replaced, so re-arming it per render would be an interop call per
    /// render to say what it already said.</summary>
    private bool _guardedAdd;

    /// <summary>The chain as it stands, by id, and the row to pick up next.
    /// Derived once when the parameters land rather than per row per render:
    /// every row needs the same answer, and computing it in a property would
    /// walk the whole list once for each row in it.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, TaskChainStatus> _statuses =
        new Dictionary<string, TaskChainStatus>(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    private string? _nextId;

    /// <summary>
    /// A view of the list, never a change to it. TaskChain reads the rows and
    /// returns statuses; nothing is written back, so the host's list is the same
    /// object it handed over.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        var statuses = new Dictionary<string, TaskChainStatus>(StringComparer.Ordinal);
        _nextId = null;

        // Nobody declared anything, so there is nothing to derive — and a list
        // that derived anyway would start marking a "next" row in every plain
        // list in the product.
        if (!TaskChain.IsChain(Tasks))
        {
            _statuses = statuses;
            return;
        }

        // One resolve, both answers. Calling TaskChain.NextReady afterwards would
        // walk the graph and detect the cycles a second time to learn something
        // the statuses in hand already say: Resolve returns them in list order, so
        // the first Ready in this loop is the row NextReady would have returned.
        foreach (var status in TaskChain.Resolve(Tasks))
        {
            statuses.TryAdd(status.Id, status);

            if (status.Readiness is not TaskReadiness.Ready) continue;

            _nextId ??= status.Id;
        }

        _statuses = statuses;
    }

    /// <summary>One row, wherever it is being drawn. Both lists render the same
    /// thing — a done row is not a different kind of row, it is the same row
    /// having been finished.</summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderRow(TaskRow task) =>
        @<TaskItem @key="task.Id"
                   Task="task"
                   Selected="@(string.Equals(SelectedId, task.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal))"
                   OnToggle="OnToggle"
                   OnSelected="OnSelected"
                   OnRename="OnRename"
                   DirectRename="@DirectRename"
                   OnBodyChanged="OnBodyChanged"
                   Actions="@RowActions"
                   QuickEdit="@QuickEdit"
                   Editing="@(QuickEdit && string.Equals(_editingId, task.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal))"
                   EditingChanged="editing => SetEditing(task.Id, editing)"
                   OnEditMove="MoveEdit"
                   AllowCopy="@AllowCopy"
                   BodyLabel="@BodyLabel"
                   Status="@StatusOf(task)"
                   Next="@(string.Equals(_nextId, task.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal))"
                   Draggable="@(Reorderable && !task.Done)"
                   Dragging="@(string.Equals(_dragging, task.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal))"
                   OnDragStart="StartDrag"
                   OnDragOver="DragOver"
                   OnDrop="Drop"
                   OnDragEnd="EndDrag"
                   RowElementId="@RowElementId(task)"
                   OnTabForward="@TabForwardFor(task)"
                   OnKeyboardMove="delta => MoveByKeyboardAsync(task, delta)"
                   TestId="@RowTestId(task)" />;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public IReadOnlyList<TaskRow> Tasks { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter]
    public string? SelectedId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnToggle { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnSelected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether rows offer to copy themselves.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowCopy { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>What a row's disclosure calls its body. Passed straight through
    /// to every row: what a body is called is the host's word for it, and one
    /// list should not have two.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string BodyLabel { get; set; } = "Body";

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<TaskRename> OnRename { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether every row's title is a field from the start rather than
    /// behind a pencil. Straight through to every row, exactly like OnRename: how a
    /// title is edited is one answer for the whole list, and a list that decided it
    /// per row would put a pencil on some titles and a caret on others.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool DirectRename { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A new task, by title. The title is all of it: minting an id, deciding where
    /// the task is stored and whether it survives a reload are the host's, exactly
    /// as saving a rename and applying a reorder are.
    /// <para>
    /// It is also what puts the add row on the list at all, and so what turns Tab
    /// off the last row into carrying on rather than stopping. Unsupplied, there is
    /// no add row and the chain ends at the bottom of the list as it always did — a
    /// list nobody asked to add tasks does not start offering to, and a field whose
    /// title nobody would hear is the same broken promise as a pencil with no
    /// <see cref="OnRename"/> behind it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Raised once per title, and never for an empty one. What arrives is trimmed,
    /// on the same rule the rename follows: a task whose title is all spaces is a
    /// row nobody can identify afterwards.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnAdd { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the add row's field is called, both to a screen reader and on
    /// the face of it: it is the accessible name and the placeholder, because it is
    /// one answer to one question and asking the host twice would invite two. Unlike
    /// every other field in this list it has no title to borrow, so it needs a name
    /// of its own — and the name is the host's word rather than this component's, for
    /// the same reason <see cref="CompletedLabel"/> is.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string AddLabel { get; set; } = "New task";

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether Tab in a rename settles it and opens the next row's, so a list can
    /// be retitled top to bottom without going back to the pencil between rows.
    /// Shift+Tab goes the other way.
    /// <para>
    /// Opt-in, because it takes a key that means something else everywhere: Tab
    /// leaves a field, and a list that took it over without being asked would be
    /// one some readers cannot get out of. A list without it is the list it was
    /// before quick edit existed, down to the markup.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// It needs <see cref="OnRename"/> to mean anything, on the same terms the
    /// pencil does: with nobody listening no row offers an editor, so there is
    /// nothing for Tab to chain.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool QuickEdit { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Straight through to every row, exactly like OnRename: whether a
    /// body can be edited is one answer for the whole list, and a list that
    /// decided it per row would be deciding which tasks are the reader's.
    /// Unsupplied, no row offers an editor.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<TaskBodyChange> OnBodyChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The host's own controls for each row, handed the task they are
    /// about. Straight through to every row for the same reason OnRename is: what a
    /// host can do to a task is one answer for the whole list, and a list that
    /// decided it per row would be deciding which tasks are actionable.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment<TaskRow>? RowActions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether finished rows move to a section of their own at the
    /// bottom. On by default: a list whose done rows stay in place is a list you
    /// read past to find the work.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool GroupCompleted { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// The host's own content between the open rows and the Completed section.
    /// <para>
    /// For the control that adds one more of whatever these are. The list owns the
    /// Completed section, so a host rendering that control after the component put
    /// it below a fold of finished work — which is the one place in the list where
    /// nothing new is going to appear.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? AfterOpenRows { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string CompletedLabel { get; set; } = "Completed";

    /// <summary>Where a row ended up: the row that moved, and the row it landed
    /// on. Applying it is the host's, because only the host knows whether the
    /// new order is saved anywhere.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<TaskMove> OnReorder { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The reader tabbed forward off the selected row.
    /// <para>
    /// Offered for the selected row alone, because it is the only row a host has
    /// somewhere else to send the focus to: what a host puts beside a list is
    /// about the row that is open. Every other row keeps plain Tab, which is the
    /// next row — so this narrows the hand-off to the one case where the document
    /// order is the wrong answer, rather than taking Tab away from the list.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnSelectedTabForward { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether rows can be picked up at all. Off for a list whose order
    /// is not the reader's to choose — one sorted by date, or a search result.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Reorderable { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = "Tasks";

    [Parameter]
    public string EmptyMessage { get; set; } = "Nothing here yet.";

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    private string ClassValue => string.Join(' ', new[]
    {
        "task-list",
        // Lets the rows that are not moving pull in while one is in flight, so
        // the list reads as having made room rather than as having a hole in it.
        _dragging is not null ? "task-list--dragging" : null,
        CssClass
    }.Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part)));

    /// <summary>
    /// The order to draw right now: the real one, or — while a row is being
    /// dragged over another — the order the drop would produce.
    /// <para>
    /// Previewing rather than drawing an insertion line is the difference
    /// between showing where a row would go and describing it. The reader is
    /// already looking at the list; moving it is the cheapest possible answer to
    /// "where would this land".
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// It is a view and nothing more. Tasks is untouched, and if the drag is
    /// abandoned the preview simply stops being computed — there is no state to
    /// roll back because nothing was changed.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <summary>The rows in the top list: everything, or everything unfinished
    /// when the done ones have somewhere of their own to go.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<TaskRow> Open =>
        GroupCompleted ? [.. Preview.Where(task => !task.Done)] : Preview;

    /// <summary>The finished rows, in the order they were given. Not sorted by
    /// when they were finished, because nothing here knows that.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<TaskRow> Completed =>
        GroupCompleted ? [.. Tasks.Where(task => task.Done)] : [];

    private string? CompletedTestId => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-completed";

    private string? Suffix(string part) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{part}";

    private IReadOnlyList<TaskRow> Preview =>
        _dragging is { } moved && _over is { } target && !string.Equals(moved, target, StringComparison.Ordinal)
            ? new TaskMove(moved, target).ApplyTo(Tasks, task => task.Id)
            : Tasks;

    private string? RowTestId(TaskRow task) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{task.Id}";

    /// <summary>Whether this list has an add row. Both halves matter: quick edit is
    /// what makes Tab the list's key at all, and without a listener there is nothing
    /// for a typed title to report to.</summary>
    private bool CanAdd => QuickEdit && OnAdd.HasDelegate;

    /// <summary>This row's status, or null when nothing here declared a
    /// dependency. Null rather than a Ready everyone gets by default, because
    /// "ready" said about a list nobody chained is an answer to a question the
    /// reader never asked.</summary>
    private TaskChainStatus? StatusOf(TaskRow task) =>
        _statuses.TryGetValue(task.Id, out var status) ? status : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which row has the editor, as the row itself reports it. Told rather than
    /// decided, because the row is where a rename is opened and closed.
    /// <para>
    /// A row may only put down the editor it is actually holding. By the time the
    /// field a Tab was pressed in reports itself closed the editor has usually
    /// moved on already, and honouring that late report would shut the row that
    /// just opened.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void SetEditing(string id, bool editing)
    {
        if (editing)
        {
            _editingId = id;
            return;
        }

        if (string.Equals(_editingId, id, StringComparison.Ordinal)) _editingId = null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The row a Tab hands the editor to: the next one down, or the one above for
    /// Shift+Tab, in the order the reader can see.
    /// <para>
    /// Open, not Tasks. A chain that ran through the completed section would send
    /// the caret to a row that is not on screen — folded away behind a count — and
    /// the reader would type into nothing they can see.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Neither end wraps, and a row that is not in the chain at all stops. Starting
    /// again at the top would hand the reader back the row they began with,
    /// retitled once already.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Off the bottom, with a host listening for new tasks, the caret carries on
    /// into the add row. Nothing opens — the field was already there — so this is
    /// only the list agreeing to do by hand what the browser would have done had the
    /// rename not taken the key off it. Off the top the chain ends: there is no
    /// "before the first row" to add anything to, and a reader walking back up is
    /// reading rather than extending.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void MoveEdit(TaskEditMove move)
    {
        var rows = Open;
        var from = rows.ToList().FindIndex(row => string.Equals(row.Id, move.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal));

        // Not in the chain at all: a finished row, renamed from the section at the
        // bottom, or a row the host has taken away from under the edit.
        if (from < 0)
        {
            _editingId = null;
            return;
        }

        var next = from + (move.Forward ? 1 : -1);

        if (next >= 0 && next < rows.Count)
        {
            _editingId = rows[next].Id;
            return;
        }

        _editingId = null;

        if (move.Forward && CanAdd) _focusAdd = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Everything this list needs the browser for, all of it after the render
    /// rather than at the moment it is decided: this is the one place Blazor
    /// guarantees runs with the batch already applied, and every element named here
    /// either does not exist yet or is about to be replaced anywhere earlier.
    /// <para>
    /// Three jobs and one method, because Blazor calls this hook once: anything owed
    /// after a render is owed here, whichever feature it belongs to.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The add field's guard is armed once, as soon as there is a field to arm —
    /// including for a reader who never touches the chain and just clicks into the
    /// composer. Which Tab it keeps has to be settled before the first keystroke,
    /// because a keydown that has reached C# is a keydown the browser has already
    /// acted on.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The caret is asked for only when something has just earned it. Focusing on
    /// every render would drag it back out of the row a reader is renaming, and
    /// focusing on the first would open the page with the caret in a field nobody
    /// asked to type in.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A row that has just been moved gets its focus ring back, by the same handle
    /// and for the same reason: once the host has applied the move the row is a
    /// different element, so the ring has gone and the next press would move
    /// whatever slid into the old slot. An id is the only thing that survives the
    /// re-render.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (CanAdd && !_guardedAdd)
        {
            _guardedAdd = true;

            await QuickEditField.GuardTabAsync(Js, _addFieldId, TabGuard.WhileFilled);
        }

        if (_focusAdd)
        {
            _focusAdd = false;

            await QuickEditField.FocusAsync(Js, _addFieldId);
        }

        if (_focusAfterMove is not { } target) return;

        _focusAfterMove = null;

        try
        {
            await Js.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogFocus", target);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Losing the focus ring is not worth tearing down the page. Broad on
            // purpose, and the same call is guarded the same way where the desktop
            // pane moves a sub-item: the row is still there and still usable, so
            // there is no failure here worth propagating.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The add field's keys, which are deliberately fewer than a row's.
    /// <para>
    /// Tab with something typed adds it, clears the field and keeps the caret in it,
    /// which is what makes "type a title, Tab, type a title, Tab" a thing a reader
    /// can do without their hands leaving the keys. It is the one keystroke this
    /// field takes off the browser, and it is taken in the browser rather than here
    /// — see the guard — because a keydown that has reached C# has already moved the
    /// focus ring.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Tab with the field empty does nothing at all, and that is the feature. The
    /// guard let the keystroke through, so the browser is already moving the focus on
    /// to whatever follows the list: leaving the composer is ordinary forward tabbing
    /// rather than a special exit this component has to invent, and there is nothing
    /// for it to drop the reader onto. It is also what stops a held-down Tab from
    /// filling the list with rows nobody named — the field it left is the field the
    /// next Tab finds empty.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Enter adds and stays, because with a permanent field there is nothing to
    /// close: the reader is still standing in the composer either way, so the only
    /// honest difference between it and Tab is that Tab is also allowed to leave.
    /// Empty, it does nothing — there is no task in an empty field, and Enter is not
    /// a way out of anywhere.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Shift+Tab is the browser's outright, typed in or not. Backwards out of the
    /// composer is a reader going back up the page, and there is nothing above the
    /// field for this list to hand them: the row above is about to change identity if
    /// a title is committed here, so re-opening "the last row" would re-open a
    /// different row from the one they saw. What was typed is settled on the way out,
    /// by the blur that leaving causes, which is the same promise every other field
    /// in this list makes.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnAddKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // Abandon what was typed and stay put. There is nothing to close — the
        // field is not a thing that was opened — so Escape means the one thing left
        // for it to mean, and it means it without reporting anything.
        if (e.Key == "Escape")
        {
            _addTitle = string.Empty;
            return;
        }

        if (e.Key is not ("Tab" or "Enter")) return;

        if (e.Key == "Tab" && e.ShiftKey) return;

        // The same trim the guard tests on, and it has to be: a field of spaces the
        // browser was stopped from leaving and this method then declined to act on
        // would be a field with no way out of it.
        var title = _addTitle.Trim();

        if (title.Length == 0) return;

        _addTitle = string.Empty;

        // Asked for again rather than assumed. The render that empties this field is
        // the same render that grows the list above it, and the caret staying put is
        // the whole bargain — so it is stated rather than left to the diff.
        _focusAdd = true;

        await OnAdd.InvokeAsync(title);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Leaving the field settles what was typed, the way leaving a rename does.
    /// <para>
    /// The alternative — a composer that clears itself when the reader looks away —
    /// was weighed and rejected. With a field that closed behind it there was a
    /// moment you could point at and call abandonment; a permanent composer never
    /// closes, so "abandoned" is indistinguishable from "parked while I check
    /// something", and clearing would mean a title that is still plainly on screen
    /// disappearing because the reader clicked a row to read it. Against that,
    /// committing costs a task the reader can see, complete or delete. Escape stays
    /// the one way to throw typed text away, here and in the rename directly above
    /// it — two fields side by side with opposite blur rules would be worse than
    /// either rule.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// It cannot double up with the keys, because they clear the field before they
    /// report: a blur that follows a commit finds nothing left to commit.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task CommitAddAsync()
    {
        var title = _addTitle.Trim();
        _addTitle = string.Empty;

        if (title.Length == 0) return;

        await OnAdd.InvokeAsync(title);
    }

    private void StartDrag(string id)
    {
        _dragging = id;
        _over = id;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which row the pointer is over, and so where the drop would land.
    /// <para>
    /// Dragging over the moved row itself is ignored. In the previewed order
    /// that row is already sitting where the pointer is, so honouring it would
    /// mean "put it back", the preview would snap to the real order, the row
    /// under the pointer would change, and the two would trade places for as
    /// long as the reader held still.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void DragOver(string id)
    {
        if (_dragging is null || string.Equals(id, _dragging, StringComparison.Ordinal)) return;

        _over = id;
    }

    private void EndDrag()
    {
        _dragging = null;
        _over = null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Commits what the preview was already showing. The drop reports the row
    /// the pointer was last over rather than whatever it technically landed on —
    /// the reader has been looking at the answer for as long as they held the
    /// row there, and anything else would move it somewhere they had not seen.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Drop(string _)
    {
        var moved = _dragging;
        var target = _over;
        EndDrag();

        // Never left the row it started on: a drag the reader thought better of,
        // not a move. Raising it would put a no-op into whatever the host does
        // with these, including its undo stack.
        if (moved is null || target is null || string.Equals(moved, target, StringComparison.Ordinal)) return;

        await OnReorder.InvokeAsync(new TaskMove(moved, target));
    }

    /// <summary>This row's focusable element, named so the focus can be put back
    /// on it after a move. Derived from the task id rather than generated, because
    /// the id has to be computable here — this is the only place that knows which
    /// row was just moved.</summary>
    private string RowElementId(TaskRow task) => RowElementId(task.Id);

    private string RowElementId(string taskId) => $"{_listId}-{Slug(taskId)}";

    /// <summary>Which rows are offered the forward hand-off: the selected one, and
    /// an empty callback — so <c>HasDelegate</c> is false and the row keeps plain
    /// Tab — for all the others.</summary>
    private EventCallback TabForwardFor(TaskRow task) =>
        string.Equals(SelectedId, task.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal) ? OnSelectedTabForward : default;

    /// <summary>
    /// Puts the focus back on the selected row.
    /// <para>
    /// The way back in from wherever a host sent the focus when
    /// <see cref="OnSelectedTabForward"/> fired, and the reason it is a method here
    /// rather than an id the host holds: row elements are named from a list-private
    /// prefix so two lists showing the same task ids cannot mint the same ids, and
    /// that prefix is not the host's to know.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The row's line rather than the circle in front of it, exactly like the
    /// focus restored after a keyboard move: the line is what carries the
    /// selection and where Alt+Arrow is heard.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    public async Task FocusSelectedAsync()
    {
        if (SelectedId is not { Length: > 0 } id) return;

        try
        {
            await Js.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogFocus", RowElementId(id));
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Guarded exactly as the post-move focus below is: the row is still
            // there and still usable without its focus ring.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>An id an HTML document will accept. Task ids are the host's and
    /// may hold anything; everything outside the safe set becomes a hyphen.</summary>
    private static string Slug(string id) =>
        new([.. id.Select(character =>
            char.IsLetterOrDigit(character) || character is '-' or '_' ? character : '-')]);

    /// <summary>
    /// A keyboard move: the row the reader was on, and which way they asked.
    /// <para>
    /// The neighbour comes from the order on screen rather than from Tasks, so
    /// the row swaps with the row above or below the one the reader can see. With
    /// finished rows folded into their own section those two orders differ, and
    /// using the underlying one would move a row past something invisible.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Clamped at both ends rather than wrapped. A row at the top that jumped to
    /// the bottom on one more press would be a move nobody asked for, and the
    /// reader would have to notice it happened before they could undo it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The move is raised, not applied, exactly as the drag is: the host owns the
    /// order. What is held here is only where to put the focus once the host has
    /// answered.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task MoveByKeyboardAsync(TaskRow task, int delta)
    {
        var order = Open;

        var from = -1;
        for (var index = 0; index < order.Count; index++)
        {
            if (!string.Equals(order[index].Id, task.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal)) continue;

            from = index;
            break;
        }

        if (from < 0) return;

        var to = from + delta;
        if (to < 0 || to >= order.Count) return;

        _announcement = $"Moved {task.Title} to {to + 1} of {order.Count}.";
        _focusAfterMove = RowElementId(task);

        await OnReorder.InvokeAsync(new TaskMove(task.Id, order[to].Id));
    }

}
